  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAs of 230 PM Wednesday afternoon, the persistent midlevel pattern \nremains in place with troughing across the eastern CONUS and ridging \nacross the west. A weak MCV is making its way through central KS. To \nthis point, only a small shield of midlevel cloud cover has been a \nresult of the MCV. As the afternoon progresses, mixed-layer \ninhibition will greatly erode with up to 2000 J/kg of instability \nacross portions of central and south-central KS. The weak MCV may \nprovide just enough ascent to spark a few thunderstorms through \nearly evening. Deep layer shear is quite meager at 20-25 kt and \nshould mitigate any prospects for severe weather. Any storm that \ndoes develop should dissipate with sunset this evening. \n\nThunderstorms are currently developing across the high Plains \nof CO/WY. Latest HREF guidance keeps the bulk of this \nthunderstorm activity west of the forecast area late tonight into\nthe morning hours of Thursday with the LLJ focusing near the CO\nborder. That being said, any remnant MCV and weak convergence \non the LLJ as it veers during the morning could allow for a few \nadditional thunderstorms to develop across portions of central \nor south- central KS. A similar scenario may play out Friday \nmorning with a few thunderstorms possible across central KS as \nthe LLJ veers.\n\nBy this weekend, temperatures will be on the increase as the core of \nthe midlevel ridge migrates back across the central and southern \nPlains. This will send temperatures back into the upper 90s and \n100s. Factoring in dew points in the mid-60s will produce heat \nindices in the low 100s. Uncertainty increases for Monday and \nbeyond as the midlevel ridge slides south and southeast as a \nnorthern stream shortwave trough glides across the northern Plains. \nThis will shunt a cold front towards the forecast area. Model \nguidance remains split with the timing/location of the front by \nMonday. This front could provide a focal point for \nshowers/thunderstorms and cooler temperatures Monday into\nTuesday.",
